    "content": {
     "content_type": "text",
     "parts": [
      "20/09/2026 18:18 — Four Options Compared\n\nPartner, the difference is:\n\n| Option | Claude builds now | Important work left out | My view |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **1. S0 + S2 + transport seam** | Fixes evidence/status problems, durable tenant storage, restart recovery, webhook worker and uninstall handling. Also creates a vendor-independent notification interface. | Actual email provider remains undecided. | **Best option. Maximum useful code without premature product work.** |\n| **2. S0 + S2 only** | Same critical reliability and persistence fixes as Option 1. | Notification architecture remains completely untouched. | Safe, but Claude will need another sprint later just to create the transport foundation. |\n| **3. S0 only** | Fixes shutdown defect, portable evidence and misleading story statuses. | All three major production blockers remain: persistence, webhook worker and uninstall handling. | Too small. Product remains unsafe after restart. |\n| **4. S0 + S2 + S6 app shell** | Does Option 1’s core work and also builds the visible web application. | Builds UI before completing the S1 market-demand decision and S4 differentiated closed-loop workflow. | Too early. Risk of producing a polished but generic forecast product. |\n\n### Why Option 1 is right\n\nThe “transport seam” means Claude creates a clean interface for email/notifications but does **not** choose SendGrid, AWS SES, Postmark or another vendor.\n\nTherefore:\n\n- No vendor cost or commitment now.\n- No fake email delivery.\n- The architecture is prepared for the later vendor decision.\n- Claude can test message creation, idempotency, suppression and failure handling.\n- The real provider can be connected later without rewriting the product.\n\nSelect **Option 1**. Option 4 may look more impressive because it adds screens, but it spends TEE on UI before proving exactly what differentiated workflow the screen should support."
     ]
    },
